# gitleaks suppression file - one accepted finding per line, by fingerprint.
#
# The Security workflow's secret scan (scripts/ci/secret-scan.ps1) fails on any secret
# gitleaks finds in the commit history. When a finding is a false positive or an accepted
# test fixture, add its fingerprint here (with a comment saying why) so it does not re-fail
# later runs - this is what keeps reruns a diff, not a re-litigation.
#
# A fingerprint looks like:  <commit-sha>:<file-path>:<rule-id>:<line-number>
# gitleaks prints the exact fingerprint for each finding; copy it verbatim.
#
# Example:
#   # Test fixture, not a real key - approved by @worm-brain 2026-09-18
#   0adc1e2f...:tests/Umbraco.Cli.Tests/Fixtures/sample.json:generic-api-key:12

# Test fixture, not a key: the webhook-log body in WebhookWireTests.cs (commit c6be928,
# PR #332). The matched values are the entity `key` / `webhookKey` GUIDs, which use the
# placeholder pattern `<8 hex>-1234-5678-abcd-ef0123456789` found throughout the tests.
# gitleaks generic-api-key fires on the JSON field names. Accepted 2026-09-29.
c6be928b399d1fb88f98dadbbed0e050e5e8cf24:tests/Umbraco.Cli.Tests/Wire/WebhookWireTests.cs:generic-api-key:269
